r of magnitude yes it  is hundreds of millions of dollars on an  annual basis but but you know the mere  cost in a state that has a hundred nine  billion dollar annual budget which seem  pretty insignificant when you're  discussing what a lot of these people  are done which is something they get  into later on yes so as you as you did  research and talk to people around this  story talk to us a little bit about what  you uncovered with regard to what would  be achieved via the execution of  Lawrence Bittaker in other words were  there  diffic individuals who felt that his  execution would in any way be justice  rather than revenge you know again it  basically depends on your perspective  and as I'm sure you're aware capital  punishment really just engenders a lot  of very emotional responses I think my  personal opinion is well I'm very  ambivalent about capital punishment  especially as practiced in places like  Texas and Florida and Georgia California  is very different and the thing that  it's a very rare individual only 2% of  individuals charged with homicide  basically ever are subjected they've  even a capital punishment trial so the  people who wind up on death row like  Lawrence Bittaker have done particularly  heinous things and so so the issue  becomes you know for me personally what  is the standard I mean what is the line  that you're going to cross in his case  when we basically kidnapped like for the  co-conspirator kidnapped tortured and  raped five teenage girls in a fashion  that the prosecutor who prosecuted both  men described as worse than Manson we're  talking about a level of depravity here  that's quite unusual and quite  dissimilar shall we say to say the state  of Texas what would it accomplish I mean  in a philosophical sense you know you  can have that argument I think you can  have a whole semester of a classic Cal  Berkeley to discuss that question I  can't  to me personally I find me to be so  heinous that basically you know that it  almost it's common sense that's an  individual I can by the way it's a  totally unrepentant psychopath who would  would basically that the only really I  think common sense thing to do but the  only thing that would make sense in  terms of punishment for this guy it was  made it clear that he couldn't care less  about what's good and about you know  other people is execution because that's  basically the only thing that will ever  get this person's attention yeah I don't  I don't think I agree with you in a  couple of senses number one having to  spend you know you execute this guy the  lights go off and that's it whatever  whatever punishment whatever he has to  deal with by sitting in that cell for  the rest of his life and rotting is  eliminated he is relieved of that as  opposed to saying you know but  psychopaths or not he's going to sit  there and he's going to do  nothing for the rest of his life but  putting that aside for a second Philip  when we think about this when you made  this argument of this was a particularly  heinous crime this is a serial murderer  and child killer and one of the most  interesting arguments I've heard against  what California does which is reserving  it only for the most extreme cases is  that it is devaluing to the victims of a  lone murderer in other words if you have  a murderer who killed one person and  California says that's not heinous  enough for us to put that person to  death isn't it in any in a sense  devaluing or an insult to the victims of  the families of the victims of someone  who only killed one person you're saying  if only one person died that's not  really as bad as if they killed multiple  people well I think I think that again  we live in California and surprisingly  by the way on an annual basis when you  compare it to Texas for instance Texas  in 2000 I think it was 2012 had 10 death  sentences handed out by juries in  California there were 24 so I think  though to address your specific question  if you if