rson I'm against  that number two when I look at what  other countries still have the death  penalty and what other countries have  chosen to get rid of it and I look at  what else those same countries have done  right and wrong the u.s. is really an  outlier in terms of the developed  wealthy nations and maintaining this and  to me that's a real concern and the  number three we've seen no evidence  whatsoever I mean literally no evidence  that the death penalty works as a  deterrent to people committing crimes so  I've decided for me personally that this  is an outdated retrograde practice that  serves no judicial purpose and is more  an issue of revenge rather than justice  that's me you know I totally understand  and that's that's a very eloquently  stated perspective and frankly it's a  perspective that it's all of those  things resonate with me and yet after  making a film of this kind you almost  you become so frustrated because again  when you read about people like Barnes  Bittaker you understand their  personalities it may not make any real  everything you say they potentially be  or even may may be actually true in a  sense of real practicality in terms of  what we should do and how we should  proceed again there's something about  him as an individual or others like them  that strikes such an emotional chord  that I think  I think you can understand the other  viewpoint that there are people who  don't feel overweight of course and what  you're saying actually I've admitted  that I can imagine specific situations  where someone I know is killed where I  might have an emotional reaction as you  indicated that makes me want that person  dead but I feel in the same way that you  just said confirming my suspicions that  those would be emotional reactions that  make me want revenge rather than an  application of justice by the judicial  system well the other the other aspect  to their David that my film gets into is  putting aside again the you know the two  sort of philosophical viewpoints we've  discussed the other the other aspect  that this is I was media dealing with  this issue in a state and also how is  the judiciary and the apparatus that is  in charge of this because California has  this situation where quite frankly both  the judiciary and the bureaucracy are  active in it in a fashion that is  despite anyone's philosophical viewpoint  the rules of law young state has  ratified four times by popular  referendum as for for this to be the  punishment and whether whether you agree  with that behind the scenes a small  group of the lead people have done  everything possible to frustrate that  yeah but it feel like that that argument  doesn't resonate with me because the the  anti-gay bigots make the same argument  when it comes to voting against same-sex  marriage and we can still a value we can  still say that you can't vote a popular  vote can't determine everything and  there are certain cases where things we  could just determine things are either  immoral unethical against the law  unconstitutional etc so while I'm not  making those specific arguments here the  fact that a referendum happened should  not ultimately decide what what is it is  that takes place with every single issue  but that's probably an argument for a  different day because unfortunately we  are at a time I do want to recommend to  people to film the devil and the death  penalty whatever your position about the  death penalty  Philip Gibbons this film will be of  interest to you and I can't thank you  enough for being here today it's my  pleasure and I would just like to  interject that it is available currently  on iTunes and Xbox 360 and through my  website double of the death penalty

